# Implement the approved direct-session Proton adapter

## Goal

Provide an optional direct Proton adapter only after the project has an approved
external session handoff and project-specific client identity.

## Required work

- Consume only session authority produced by the accepted external handoff.
- Never automate a browser, scrape a user profile, or collect Proton passwords.
- Detect account and server capabilities instead of assuming a plan or rollout.
- Implement bounded timeouts, cancellation, retry policy, backoff, concurrency,
  session refresh, and structured provider error conversion.
- Convert messages, conversations, labels, drafts, events, and attachments into
  provider-neutral domain types.
- Preserve required client-side cryptography without logging decrypted content
  or key material.
- Fail closed when the observed protocol changes.

## Acceptance criteria

- A project-specific Proton client/partner identity is documented and approved.
- Every advertised capability has a tested implementation or is omitted.
- Provider-specific types, URLs, session tokens, and cryptographic material do
  not cross into the capability domain or public tool schemas.
- No browser automation runtime is part of install, login, or mail transport.
- A gated live account can perform every capability advertised by this adapter.

## Evidence

- Provider registration and handoff evidence
- Adapter contract and protocol-fixture tests
- Capability-negotiation matrix
- Session-refresh and provider-change regression tests
- Redacted live-account adapter transcript
